+# The cluster's SLO: who is behind, who is flapping, and what it costs
+
+A service's SLO is about latency and errors. A cluster's SLO is about *agreement*: how many writes the
+cluster promised to commit, and how many it actually did. This page is the project's version, and the
+counters it is computed from are the ones only a cluster has.
+
+## The counters
+
+| counter | what it answers |
+| --- | --- |
+| lag per peer | how far behind the leader's commit each follower is |
+| elections stood / won | who is flapping, and who keeps winning |
+| refusals | writes this node refused because no majority was reachable |
+| commits | writes this node saw committed |
+| applied | how far this node's state machine has got |
+
+The first and last are two views of the same fact: the difference between nodes' applied indexes *is*
+the lag, seen from each end. A node whose lag grows is the node that will be last to notice a leader
+change, and the first to be a problem when one happens. A node that stands for election over and over
+while another keeps winning is a node whose clock or network is the problem — and a node that refuses
+writes while committing none is the one that was on the wrong side of a partition.
+
+## The SLO, in counts
+
+An objective is a promise about a ratio, and the **error budget** is how much of it the cluster has
+spent:
+
+```text
+budget_left = attempts × (1 − objective) − failures
+```
+
+Everything is an integer and everything is a count, because a budget that needs a clock is a budget
+that moves when the machine does. A 99.9% objective over a thousand attempts allows exactly one
+failure; over ten thousand it allows ten. That is the arithmetic a release decision is made of: "we
+have five failures of budget left this window" is a sentence somebody can act on.
+
+## What it does not say
+
+The counters are per node, and they are deliberately not a global truth: a node's view of a peer's lag
+is as fresh as the last message it received. The audit (`5.22`) is the step that compares the nodes'
+views *against each other*; this page is what each node says about itself, which is what an operator
+reads first.
